    <string name="settings_help_url_title">WordPress site address</string>
    <string name="settings_help_url_text">The address where your blog can be reached in the browser, e.g. “https://udehne.de”.\n\nEasiest: open your blog in the browser and copy the address from the address bar – without “/wp-admin” or subpages. The app adds “https://” itself if needed.</string>
    <string name="settings_help_user_text">Your WordPress login name – the name you use to log in at “/wp-admin” (not the display name).\n\nYou\'ll find it in WordPress under Users → Profile → “Username” (at the very top, grey, can\'t be changed). The user needs at least the “Author” role.</string>
    <string name="settings_help_password_text">Not your normal WordPress password, but a separate password just for this app.\n\nHow to create it:\n1. Log in to WordPress in your browser (…/wp-admin).\n2. On the left, go to Users → Profile.\n3. Scroll all the way down to “Application Passwords”.\n4. Enter a name, e.g. “WPoster phone”, and tap “Add New Application Password”.\n5. Copy the password shown (24 characters in 6 groups) and paste it here. It\'s only shown once; spaces don\'t matter.\n\nIf the section is missing, the feature is disabled on your site (e.g. by a security plugin). You can revoke the password at any time in the same place.</string>
    <string name="settings_help_image_title">Image size</string>
    <string name="settings_help_image_text">Photos are scaled down to this maximum edge length before uploading. This saves time, mobile data and storage space on the server.\n\n2560 px (default) is plenty for blog photos – even on large screens. Location (GPS) and other metadata are always removed.</string>
    <string name="settings_help_upload_title">Maximum file size</string>
    <string name="settings_help_upload_text">The largest file your server accepts. The app rejects larger videos before uploading.\n\nYou\'ll find it in WordPress under Media → Add New Media File, where it says “Maximum upload file size: … MB”. Enter that value here.</string>
